{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\ngit diff --stat -- docs/design/dao.stream.md\ngit diff -- docs/design/dao.stream.md\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '620,735p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\ngit diff --stat -- docs/design/dao.stream.md\ngit diff -- docs/design/dao.stream.md\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '620,735p'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n docs/design/dao.stream.md | 96 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----\n 1 file changed, 87 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.stream.md b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\nindex 121a759..2b09e1f 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n@@ -443,8 +443,8 @@ A transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n excludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n impossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n-evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never\n-as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n+evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n+spans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n that cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n exclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n \n@@ -493,6 +493,23 @@ A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n+- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n+  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n+  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n+  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n+  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n+  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n+  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n+  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n+- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n+  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n+  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n+  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n+  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n+  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n+  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n+  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n+  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n - Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n@@ -647,15 +664,71 @@ positions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n eviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n absence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n reader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n-deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n-told what it missed.\n+deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n+receives a `gap`.\n \n When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n-skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it\n-missed values.\n+skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n+eviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n+handle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n+that must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n+\n+### Complete history\n+\n+Some consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n+interpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n+not merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n+such a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n+\n+**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n+declaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n+the transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n+(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n+a locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n+configuration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n+added. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n+that declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n+\n+**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n+origin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n+`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n+the history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n+\n+Both stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n+requirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n+is the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n+a consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n+cursor is right and sufficient.\n+\n+**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n+same kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n+a window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n+happened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n+Capacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n+and never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n+\n+The obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n+complete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n+evicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n+every value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n+it for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n+retained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n+suffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n+`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\n+\n+What it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\n+transport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\n+one, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\n+returns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n+and evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n+given up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n+logically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n+`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite: unexpected exhaustion of\n+the underlying medium is `transport-error`, never the eviction of acknowledged\n+history.\n \n ## Composition\n \n@@ -673,9 +746,9 @@ yielding execution as needed by the host runtime.\n Flow control (a reader pausing a sender) is an interpreter's concern. A pause\n has to be a lease and not a switch to avoid permanently stuck states. Those\n semantics live in `dao.lease.md`, whose facts are datoms on a medium\n-(`dao.space`). DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a reader that falls\n-behind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader decides what\n-that means.\n+(`dao.space`). DaoStream provides the honest baseline: a cursor that falls\n+behind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader holding it\n+decides what that means.\n \n ## Explicitly Absent\n \n@@ -702,6 +775,11 @@ Absent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n - **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n - **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n - **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n+- **a retention or completeness predicate** — retention is declared, through\n+  the transport's nature and the creation specification. Asking a handle would\n+  be the same defect as asking whether it is closed: the answer is\n+  configuration the composition already holds, and reading it back at runtime\n+  invites code that branches on what it should have been wired with.\n \n ## The v2 namespace is transient\n \n   620\t  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\n   621\t- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a\n   622\t  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n   623\t\n   624\tThere is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are\n   625\tauthoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.\n   626\t\n   627\t## Concurrency\n   628\t\n   629\tEach logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer\n   630\tsurface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream\n   631\titself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see\n   632\tWriting). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position\n   633\tin the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's\n   634\toutcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing\n   635\tfurther about concurrent operations.\n   636\t\n   637\tThis follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one\n   638\ttruth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\n   639\treader that observes\n   640\t`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n   641\tcontradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global\n   642\tview exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is\n   643\tanswered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a\n   644\tposition in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from\n   645\tthis handle, the answer is read where it is.\n   646\tWhat an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —\n   647\ta merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,\n   648\tand interpretation happens above the stream.\n   649\t\n   650\t## Retention and Gaps\n   651\t\n   652\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   653\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   654\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   655\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   656\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   657\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   658\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   659\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   660\t\n   661\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   662\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   663\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   664\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   665\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   666\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   667\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   668\treceives a `gap`.\n   669\t\n   670\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   671\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   672\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   673\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   674\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   675\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   676\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   677\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   678\t\n   679\t### Complete history\n   680\t\n   681\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   682\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   683\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   684\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   685\t\n   686\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   687\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   688\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   689\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   690\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   691\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   692\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   693\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   694\t\n   695\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   696\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   697\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   698\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   699\t\n   700\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   701\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   702\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   703\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   704\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   705\t\n   706\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   707\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   708\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   709\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   710\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   711\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   712\t\n   713\tThe obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n   714\tcomplete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n   715\tevicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n   716\tevery value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n   717\tit for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n   718\tretained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n   719\tsuffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n   720\t`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\n   721\t\n   722\tWhat it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\n   723\ttransport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\n   724\tone, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\n   725\treturns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n   726\tand evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n   727\tgiven up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n   728\tlogically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n   729\t`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite: unexpected exhaustion of\n   730\tthe underlying medium is `transport-error`, never the eviction of acknowledged\n   731\thistory.\n   732\t\n   733\t## Composition\n   734\t\n   735\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
